index ddcdc217..cba50bed 100644
--- a/docs/development-logging.md
+++ b/docs/development-logging.md
@@ -39,7 +39,7 @@ Rules that apply to brain diagnostics specifically:
 
 - The panel is often closed. Events go into a bounded queue (oldest dropped, drop count reported), are replayed exactly once when the panel reconnects, and are de-duplicated by sequence number, so a closed panel never means a silently lost failure.
 - The panel re-sends every pending request on a timer (image generation retries every 5s for up to 28 minutes), so suppression logs for a re-send must pass a `repeatKey` (normally the `reqId`) to `diagSkip`: the first occurrence is recorded and later repeats stay silent. A visible warning must not become hundreds of identical entries.
-- Failure paths must not embed foreign text. Host replies and exception messages can echo the prompt, a filename, or user content, and `warn`/`error` entries are uploaded by the remote debug reporter, so pass them through `diagReason`: it records `reason=<category> len=<n> sig=<hash>` instead of the original string. The raw message still travels to the host and the panel UI unchanged; only the diagnostic log is summarised.
+- Failure paths must not embed foreign text. Host replies and exception messages can echo the prompt, a filename, user content — or the user's API key — and `warn`/`error` entries are uploaded by the remote debug reporter. For the Mivo generation channel (2026-08-05, incident follow-up) the rule is strict: log **only the fixed outcome code** (`code=MIVO_…` from the `GEN_CODE` closed set). Do not log the raw message *or any derivative of it* — `diagReason`'s `reason=<category> len=<n> sig=<hash>` summary is itself a side channel (length reveals key length, the hash is a stable fingerprint), so it is no longer acceptable on these paths. The raw message must also never travel to the panel UI or BroadcastChannel replies on this channel: the reply carries the outcome code and a fixed i18n message. (`diagReason` remains in use on legacy non-credential paths pending migration.)
 - Diagnostic messages describe what happened, they do not carry payloads. `redactDiag` replaces any URL (including `cindy-media://` media addresses) and HTML fragment and truncates the line; prompts are recorded as `promptChars=<n>` and media identity as an 8-character hash prefix. Never concatenate a raw prompt, media URL, or card HTML into a diagnostic message.
 - Chat card transport (`card` slot) follows the same three paths: a version actually handed to the host logs, a middle version dropped by the local coalescer logs a warning that says `coalesced locally` (never blame the host, which only silently drops over-sends), and a host rejection or send exception logs an error. Success wording states that the send attempt completed — the host may still rate-limit or sanitise the version, so never claim delivery.
 - A tool call that draws a card owns the whole visual outcome: the chat no longer renders the default media card for it. Every failure path must therefore converge into one `state:'done'` failure card on the same card slot (localised copy per error class, no `working` animation classes left behind) and only then submit `tool-result(ok:false, errorCode)`. Internal details stay in the diagnostic log; the card shows the desensitised reason plus what the user can do next. Plugin error codes must not reuse the host-reserved ones (`INTERNAL` / `TIMEOUT` / `GHOST_*`).
